@page
@{
    var hour = DateTime.Now.Hour;
}

<p>
    HTML and CSS make a page that looks the same no matter what. To make a page that can count,
    make choices, or change, you have to use a programming language. This website uses <strong>C#</strong>.
</p>
<p>
    The C# language runs on the server. It builds the HTML, and then the server sends your
    HTML code to your browser. The browser does not see the C# code but only the HTML and CSS code.
</p>

<h2>What is razor?</h2>
<p>
    razor lets you put C# and HTML in the same place it uses the asp.net system to let this happen. Razor files end in
    <code>.cshtml</code>.Which is a mashup of C# and HTML I feel dumb for not realising that sooner. The most important thing to remember is that the @@ symbol switches from HTML to C#.
    
    </p>

<h2>Showing values for websites</h2>
<pre>
@@{
    var name = "Bob";
}
&lt;p&gt;Hi, @@name!&lt;/p&gt;
</pre>
<p>
    The <code>@@{ }</code> line holds C# code inside it. <code>@@name</code> puts the value of the variable
    into the pages code. The browser gets the code and then puts it in the blank in the code.
</p>
<p>
    For example,I made this page do it. the computers clock says <strong>@DateTime.Now.ToString("h:mm tt")</strong>.
    If you refresh the page and the time would change to the current time.
</p>

<h2>code choices with if</h2>
<pre>
@@if (hour &lt; 12)
{
    &lt;p&gt;Good morning!&lt;/p&gt;
}
else
{
    &lt;p&gt;Good afternoon!&lt;/p&gt;
}
</pre>
<p>
    Right now this should say:
    @if (hour < 12)
    {
        <strong>Good morning!</strong>
    }
    else
    {
        <strong>Good afternoon!</strong>
    }
</p>


<h2>Files and web addresses</h2>

<p>Each file in the <code>Pages</code> folder becomes part of a web address like:</p>
<table>
    <tr><th>File</th><th>Address</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Pages/Index.cshtml</code></td><td><code>/</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Pages/Lessons/Html.cshtml</code></td><td><code>/Lessons/Html</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>Pages/Lessons/Razor.cshtml</code></td><td><code>/Lessons/Razor</code></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Layouts</h2>
<p>
    The menus and options are not on every single page. They are actually in one file,
    <code>Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml</code>. The line <code>@@RenderBody()</code> is where the content for each
    page goes. The parts also use <code>Pages/Lessons/_LessonLayout.cshtml</code>,
    which adds the part title and the Back and Next links that you use to go through them. Confused me a lot at first.
</p>

<p class="note">
    <strong>Showing an @@ sign:</strong> Because @@ means "C# starts here," you type it twice
    (<code>@@@@</code>) to show one on the page.Also meaning I had to add 
    <code>@@@@@@@@</code> signs to show to and eight for the last four.
</p>
